<section class="sub" id="hp-findings">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">01 · SANTA FE'S OWN FINDINGS</div>
<h2 class="section-title">2021 Continuity and Connection Report</h2>
</div>
<div class="callout quotecall">
<h4>Continuity and Connection: A Pre-Planning Report on the State of Historic Preservation in Santa Fe</h4>
<p>Prepared by the City of Santa Fe Historic Preservation Division with the New Mexico Health Equity Partnership, Littleglobe, John Murphey (Architectural History Services), and Six Blooms, with National Park Service grant support (March 2021). It's a pre-planning study, not an adopted plan — the Governing Body has not formally acted on it and it isn't currently linked from the Historic Preservation Division's webpage, so its four findings below are best treated as open background research for this General Plan and Land Development Code update, not settled city policy.</p>
<p><strong>Documentation has lapsed.</strong> The city's own systematic surveys of its five historic districts stopped after 2002. Since then, "the burden of producing or updating historic building inventories" has fallen entirely on property owners as designation or review comes up, against a public status map the report itself calls "often incorrect."</p>
<p><strong>Authority is unclear.</strong> The report can't say with confidence whether the City Council must still ratify a status change once the Historic Districts Review Board makes it, and notes board members "receive very little if any training" in the significance criteria they're applying — while the city code never formally defines "significance" at all.</p>
<p><strong>Preservation and displacement are in tension, not resolved.</strong> In the report's own words, Santa Fe's historic districts are "contested places... defined for many residents as much by the families and businesses that have moved away as by the old buildings," with legacy families "increasingly displaced by part-time residents and vacation rental occupants." The General Plan's Policy 2-G-5 already names this directly, calling for "close coordination" between preserving character and providing affordable housing.</p>
<p><strong>Whose heritage counts is narrower than it should be.</strong> The report traces Santa Fe's regulatory style to a deliberate "synthetic combination" of Spanish-colonial and Pueblo forms, assembled in the 1910s for tourism promotion and statehood politics — not organic preservation of the city's actually diverse building fabric, including the Hispanic vernacular architecture of neighborhoods like Westside-Guadalupe.</p>
<p class="muted" style="margin-top:8px;">Source: <a href="https://santafenm.gov/media/ordinances_resolutions/1a_-_Continuity___Connection_Final_Report_interactive-compressed.pdf">Continuity and Connection, City of Santa Fe (March 2021)</a> ↗ · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HC-jaTYIdnQ">recorded interviews conducted alongside the report</a> ↗</p>
</div>
<p class="section-note" style="margin-top:14px;">The eight cities below aren't a general best-of list — each one was chosen because it speaks to one of these four findings directly.</p>
</section>
<section class="sub" id="hp-survey-group">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">02 · SURVEY &amp; DOCUMENTATION</div>
<h2 class="section-title">Closing the documentation gap</h2>
<p class="section-note">Every other preservation function — design review, incentive eligibility, National Register nominations — depends on knowing what's out there and why it matters. Los Angeles shows what closing that gap at full scale looks like.</p>
</div>
<div class="case-study-list">
<article class="card case-study" id="hp-losangeles">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>Los Angeles, CA</h3>
<span class="cs-tag">Comprehensive citywide survey</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>SurveyLA, the city's first-ever comprehensive historic resources survey, ran from a 2009 pilot through completion in 2017 and covered all 880,000 parcels across the city's roughly 500 square miles. Before it, only about 15% of Los Angeles had ever been surveyed for historic resources — a gap of the same basic kind Santa Fe's report describes, at a much larger scale.</p>
<span class="field-label">Approach</span>
<p>Rather than survey block by block for individual landmark eligibility alone, the city first wrote its first-ever citywide historic context statement, then organized fieldwork around it, using a tablet-based data-collection tool (the Field Guide Survey System) to keep thousands of parcel evaluations consistent across multiple teams and years — the kind of standardized methodology Santa Fe's report says its own property-by-property process currently lacks.</p>
<span class="field-label">Results</span>
<p>The context statement includes dedicated, separately published studies for specific communities' heritage — including Latino Los Angeles and Japanese American history — alongside conventional architectural and thematic contexts, rather than evaluating every property against one default architectural narrative. The project won the LA Conservancy's 2017 Chairman's Award on completion.</p>
<span class="field-label">Considerations</span>
<p class="muted">An undertaking at this scale is a dedicated, multi-year budget commitment, not a policy change. Santa Fe's entire Historic Preservation Division is a director, two senior planners, a planner, a planner technician, and an inspections/enforcement officer, per its own report — worth sizing a survey resumption to that capacity rather than treating SurveyLA as a template to copy wholesale.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row">
<a href="https://www.laconservancy.org/surveyla-the-los-angeles-historic-resources-survey/">LA Conservancy, on SurveyLA ↗</a>
<a href="https://planning.lacity.gov/preservation-design/historic-resources-survey">LA City Planning, Historic Resources Survey ↗</a>
</div>
</article>
</div>
</section>
<section class="sub" id="hp-tiers-group">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">03 · PROTECTION TIERS &amp; AUTHORITY</div>
<h2 class="section-title">Options short of full designation, and what happens without clear authority</h2>
<p class="section-note">Santa Fe's report flags a code that never formally defines "significance" and can't say for certain whether Council must ratify an HDRB decision. San Antonio shows a tier of protection between nothing and full landmark status; Charlotte shows what a missing authority step actually costs.</p>
</div>
<div class="case-study-list">
<article class="card case-study" id="hp-sanantonio">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>San Antonio, TX <a href="/cities/sanantonio/" style="font-size:0.7em;font-weight:500;">— full profile ↩</a></h3>
<span class="cs-tag">Tiered conservation system</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>Full historic districts and individual landmarks sit alongside lighter-touch Neighborhood Conservation Districts (NCDs) — a middle tier between no protection and full design review, first used in Alta Vista (2003).</p>
<span class="field-label">Approach</span>
<p>A "10-Year Tax Freeze" waives city property tax for 5 years after a qualifying rehabilitation (at least 30% of the improvement's value), then taxes only 50% of the post-rehab value for years 6–10 — automatic for qualifying landmark and district properties, not a competitive grant. New historic-district homeowners separately get a flat 20% city tax exemption for 10 years (15 if they stay the full term).</p>
<span class="field-label">Results</span>
<p>469 properties have used the rehabilitation incentive since 2002, across 13 zip codes citywide; the average participant saves roughly $4,800 a year in city property taxes.</p>
<span class="field-label">Considerations</span>
<p class="muted">Some owners describe the incentive application process as time-consuming enough to be a participation barrier, by the city's own reporting. Alta Vista's NCD needed a 2018 ordinance amendment — 15 years after its 2003 creation — because the original guidelines had become outdated, a reminder that a conservation-district tool needs periodic recalibration, not a one-time adoption.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row">
<a href="https://www.sa.gov/Directory/Departments/OHP/Landmarks-Heritage/Incentives">City of San Antonio, OHP incentives ↗</a>
<a href="https://sanantonioreport.org/san-antonio-city-tax-breaks-historic-property-owners-king-william/">San Antonio Report ↗</a>
</div>
</article>
<article class="card case-study" id="hp-charlotte">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>Charlotte, NC <a href="/cities/charlotte/" style="font-size:0.7em;font-weight:500;">— full profile ↩</a></h3>
<span class="cs-tag">Automatic incentive · governance gap</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>The Charlotte-Mecklenburg Historic Landmarks Commission, created in 1973 as a joint city-county body, is one of the oldest local preservation commissions in this library's set — unusual for spanning both a city and its surrounding county.</p>
<span class="field-label">Approach</span>
<p>Landmark designation triggers an automatic 50% deferral of property taxes — self-executing, with no separate application or competitive award — plus Certificate of Appropriateness design review and up to a one-year delay on any demolition request. If a landmarked property is later de-designated, the deferred taxes become repayable, discouraging owners from seeking the tax benefit only to abandon designation afterward.</p>
<span class="field-label">Results</span>
<p>472 properties currently hold landmark designation.</p>
<span class="field-label">Considerations</span>
<p class="muted">A Charlotte Urban Institute review found that 37% of landmarked or surveyed historic properties had been demolished or significantly altered over time regardless of designation; in the Fourth Ward, only 4 of 27 original blocks retain their historic building stock. In 2024, the Historic Landmarks Commission recommended landmark status for the Steele Creek Manse, but city staff never forwarded that recommendation to Council for a vote — the building was demolished before it received the protection its own review board had recommended. This is exactly the kind of gap Santa Fe's own report flags as unresolved in its process: an automatic tax deferral, or any protection, only works once a recommendation is required to reach a final decision-maker, not left as a discretionary next step.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row">
<a href="https://ui.charlotte.edu/">UNC Charlotte Urban Institute ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.axios.com/local/charlotte/2024/historic-preservation-charlotte">Axios Charlotte, on the Steele Creek Manse ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.wfae.org/local-news">WFAE, local coverage ↗</a>
</div>
</article>
</div>
</section>
<section class="sub" id="hp-displacement-group">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">04 · PRESERVATION &amp; DISPLACEMENT</div>
<h2 class="section-title">The tension Santa Fe's own General Plan already names</h2>
<p class="section-note">Policy 2-G-5 calls for "close coordination" between preserving character and providing affordable housing; the report describes legacy families displaced even as the buildings they lived in are preserved. The four cities below work on different sides of that same problem — one lets a historic building's foregone density get built elsewhere, one funds the small businesses most at risk of being priced out, and two are honest examples of what preservation success alone does not solve.</p>
</div>
<div class="case-study-list">
<article class="card case-study" id="hp-miami">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>Miami, FL <a href="/cities/miami/" style="font-size:0.7em;font-weight:500;">— full profile ↩</a></h3>
<span class="cs-tag">Trading density, not just restricting it</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>The MiMo (Miami Modern) Biscayne Boulevard Historic District, designated in 2006, protects 1950s–60s roadside and motel-strip commercial architecture — a period of significance from 1923 to 1967, and a deliberately broader preservation target than the single architectural style most historic districts protect.</p>
<span class="field-label">Approach</span>
<p>The district pairs its design guidelines, including a 35-foot height limit, with a market-based transfer-of-development-rights (TDR) tool: owners of historic buildings in eligible areas can sell their unused base development rights to developers seeking extra density nearby. That's a direct financial answer to the exact tension Santa Fe's Policy 2-G-5 names — a historic building's foregone density doesn't just disappear, it gets built (and adds housing capacity) somewhere else nearby.</p>
<span class="field-label">Results</span>
<p>The Vagabond Motel, built in the 1950s, was restored and reopened as the Vagabond Hotel in 2014, preserving its original terrazzo flooring and landmark sign — a flagship adaptive-reuse example.</p>
<span class="field-label">Considerations</span>
<p class="muted">Later proposals to raise the district's 35-foot height limit to 55 feet drew organized resident opposition — a common tension in incentive-based preservation: once a height ceiling becomes negotiable for one project, neighbors often lose confidence it will hold as a real limit.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row">
<a href="https://www.miami21.org/PublicBenefits_HistoricPreservation.asp">Miami 21 — historic preservation TDR ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.miaminewtimes.com/arts/inside-the-vagabond-hotel-biscayne-boulevards-restored-mimo-gem-photos-6495935">Miami New Times, the Vagabond Hotel ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.biscaynetimes.com/news/upper-east-side-residents-haggle-over-mimo/">Biscayne Times, residents on MiMo height limits ↗</a>
</div>
</article>
<article class="card case-study" id="hp-sanfrancisco">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>San Francisco, CA</h3>
<span class="cs-tag">Funding the people, not just the buildings</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>The Legacy Business Registry, created by voter-approved Proposition J in November 2015, is aimed at exactly the population Santa Fe's report describes losing ground — long-standing community institutions being priced out of the neighborhoods that gave them their history, independent of whether the building itself is protected.</p>
<span class="field-label">Approach</span>
<p>A business qualifies after 30+ years of continuous San Francisco operation (or 20+ years if it's both culturally significant and at significant displacement risk), a demonstrated contribution to neighborhood history or identity, and a commitment to maintaining the physical features or traditions that define it. Registered businesses become eligible for a Rent Stabilization Grant — up to $4.50 per square foot per year, capped at $22,500 annually — funded through the Legacy Business Historic Preservation Fund.</p>
<span class="field-label">Results</span>
<p>More than 300 businesses are on the registry. Through May 2022, the city had paid out roughly $2.68 million in rent-stabilization grants to 49 businesses in the program's first grant year, averaging about $15,700 per business.</p>
<span class="field-label">Considerations</span>
<p class="muted">This protects tenancy and operating cost, not ownership or the underlying real estate — a registered business can still lose its lease if a landlord chooses not to renew, and the grant amounts are calibrated to San Francisco commercial rents, not necessarily transferable dollar-for-dollar to a smaller market. The mechanism is what's transferable: treating displacement of long-standing occupants as a preservation problem in its own right, worth a funded program, not just a side effect to note.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row">
<a href="https://sfplanning.org/project/legacy-business-registry">SF Planning, Legacy Business Registry ↗</a>
<a href="https://media.api.sf.gov/documents/LBP_Grants_Annual_Report_2021-22.pdf">Legacy Business Program, FY 2021–22 grants report ↗</a>
</div>
</article>
<article class="card case-study" id="hp-charleston">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>Charleston, SC <a href="/cities/charleston/" style="font-size:0.7em;font-weight:500;">— full profile ↩</a></h3>
<span class="cs-tag">The original model</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>Charleston's Old and Historic District, created by ordinance in 1931, is generally credited as the first historic-district zoning ordinance in the United States. The Board of Architectural Review (BAR) it established had no precedent to follow.</p>
<span class="field-label">Approach</span>
<p>The BAR's design-review model — a board with Certificate-of-Appropriateness-style approval — became the template later copied nationwide, including by most other cities in this library. Historic Charleston Foundation separately ran a revolving-fund and preservation-easement rehabilitation program in the Ansonborough neighborhood starting in 1959, pairing regulatory protection with a market-based rehab mechanism a full generation before that became a common preservation-finance model elsewhere.</p>
<span class="field-label">Results</span>
<p>The BAR's authority was tested and held during Hurricane Hugo recovery in 1989, when the board unanimously declined to relax standards for storm-damaged buildings. The district now protects more than 4,800 historic structures — up from 1,168 in a 1944 survey and 2,800+ in a 1974 survey — after a 1967 expansion roughly tripled its size.</p>
<span class="field-label">Considerations</span>
<p class="muted">Charleston is a frequently cited case study of preservation-driven tourism pressure — the peninsula's residential population declined for decades before recently beginning to recover, a trend local reporting links partly to tourism and short-term-rental pressure inside the historic core. It's the same dynamic Santa Fe's report describes in its own words, at a different scale.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row">
<a href="https://www.preservationsociety.org/past-psc-advocacy-wins-1931-zoning-ordinance/">Preservation Society of Charleston ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.nps.gov/articles/charleston-and-preservation.htm">National Park Service ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.postandcourier.com/boomandbalance/charleston-peninsula-gains-residents-first-time-since-1930s/article_58deeab7-2af2-4082-ad8f-bb9bd3df2a32.html">Post and Courier, peninsula population trend ↗</a>
</div>
</article>
<article class="card case-study" id="hp-savannah">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>Savannah, GA <a href="/cities/savannah/" style="font-size:0.7em;font-weight:500;">— full profile ↩</a></h3>
<span class="cs-tag">Private, self-renewing finance</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>Historic Savannah Foundation's Revolving Fund is a nonprofit mechanism, not a city ordinance — market-based preservation finance operating alongside the city's historic-district zoning rather than only government regulation.</p>
<span class="field-label">Approach</span>
<p>The Foundation buys vacant, blighted, or endangered historic buildings with privately raised capital, stabilizes them, then resells them through a competitive process to preservation-minded buyers with a permanent preservation easement attached to the deed at sale. Proceeds from each sale recycle back into the fund to buy the next building, converting one-time philanthropic capital into a continuously renewing acquisition tool.</p>
<span class="field-label">Results</span>
<p>420 buildings saved and protected since the fund's inception.</p>
<span class="field-label">Considerations</span>
<p class="muted">The National Park Service downgraded its assessment of Savannah's historic district in 2018, calling it the most threatened it had ever been, citing hotel development: 57 hotels and inns now operate within roughly one square mile of the historic district, with more planned, and the city drew 17 million visitors in 2022. Long-time residents report displacement pressure and quality-of-life strain — a well-funded, successful preservation program doesn't by itself resolve the tourism pressure that success can attract.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row">
<a href="http://www.myhsf.org/available-properties/revolving-fund/">Historic Savannah Foundation ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.wtoc.com/2024/01/04/threatened-district-is-development-diminishing-savannahs-character/">WTOC ↗</a>
</div>
</article>
</div>
</section>
<section class="sub" id="hp-community-group">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">05 · COMMUNITY VOICE</div>
<h2 class="section-title">Who drives a designation decision</h2>
<p class="section-note">Santa Fe's report flags COVID-era limits on community engagement as unfinished business for its next planning phase. Seattle is a reminder of what's possible when residents, not only a review board, drive the outcome.</p>
</div>
<div class="case-study-list">
<article class="card case-study" id="hp-seattle">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>Seattle, WA <a href="/cities/seattle/" style="font-size:0.7em;font-weight:500;">— full profile ↩</a></h3>
<span class="cs-tag">Citizen-led designation</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>The Pike Place Market Historical District was created by a 1971 citizen ballot initiative, after voters overturned a 1963 urban-renewal plan that would have demolished most of the market. A "Friends of the Market" campaign, led by architect Victor Steinbrueck, put the district's protection directly to a public vote rather than leaving it to a council decision.</p>
<span class="field-label">Approach</span>
<p>A 12-member citizen Historical Commission reviews changes within the district. The Pike Place Market Preservation and Development Authority (PDA), created in 1973, owns roughly 80% of the properties in the district — a public-development-authority ownership model rather than regulation spread across many private owners, giving the preservation body direct control over maintenance and tenanting, not only design-review veto power.</p>
<span class="field-label">Results</span>
<p>The market remains a full working district rather than a museum piece — about 500 residents, roughly 90% of them low-income seniors, live in four named buildings within it (Livingston Baker, Sanitary Market, LaSalle, and Stewart House), alongside its commercial stalls. 2023 figures: 20.9 million visitors and $177 million in commercial sales.</p>
<span class="field-label">Considerations</span>
<p class="muted">Research for this library did not surface a well-documented account of a major trade-off or controversy here comparable to this library's other preservation case studies — worth noting honestly rather than implying one exists. The PDA's ownership model and citizen-commission review may simply generate less public friction, or that friction may be thinly documented in secondary sources.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row">
<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pike_Place_Market">Wikipedia, Pike Place Market ↗ (used as the practical secondary source here; the market's and PDA's own pages did not yield independently citable detail in this research pass)</a>
</div>
</article>
</div>
</section>
<section class="sub" id="hp-standards-group">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">06 · SHPO / CLG STANDARDS</div>
<h2 class="section-title">Santa Fe's code, checked against New Mexico's own preservation standards</h2>
</div>
<div class="callout quotecall">
<h4>Why this comparison is different from the eight above</h4>
<p>Santa Fe is one of ten communities currently listed as a <a href="https://www.nmhistoricpreservation.org/programs/clg.html">Certified Local Government (CLG)</a> ↗ by New Mexico's Historic Preservation Division (HPD), the office that carries out State Historic Preservation Officer (SHPO) functions for the state. CLG status is a joint state/National Park Service certification that makes a city eligible for dedicated preservation grant funding, in exchange for meeting a set of program standards — most of them drawn from or cross-referencing the Secretary of the Interior's Standards — laid out in HPD's <a href="https://nmhistoricpreservation.org/assets/files/clg/NM%20CLG%20Manual%20-%202020.pdf">2020 CLG Manual</a> ↗. Unlike the eight cities above, this section isn't asking what another city does well; it's checking Santa Fe's own Land Development Code (Chapter 14) against a program Santa Fe already belongs to. Some of what follows is a certification requirement; some is a suggested practice the manual leaves to local discretion — each item below says which.</p>
<p class="muted" style="margin-top:8px;">The Secretary of the Interior's Standards themselves are federal, issued by the National Park Service (NPS) — the CLG Manual applies them locally but doesn't reproduce them. Each item below that turns on one links directly to the NPS original alongside the CLG Manual page: <a href="https://www.nps.gov/subjects/historicpreservation/standards.htm">full NPS standards index</a> ↗ · <a href="https://www.nps.gov/articles/sec-standards-prof-quals.htm">Professional Qualifications Standards</a> ↗ · <a href="https://www.nps.gov/subjects/taxincentives/secretarys-standards-rehabilitation.htm">Standards for Rehabilitation</a> ↗ · <a href="https://www.nps.gov/articles/sec_stds_id_standards.htm">Standards for Identification</a> ↗ · <a href="https://www.nps.gov/articles/sec_stds_eval_stds.htm">Standards for Evaluation</a> ↗</p>
</div>
<div class="case-study-list" style="margin-top:14px;">
<article class="card case-study" id="hp-shpo-board">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>Historic Districts Review Board qualifications</h3>
<span class="cs-tag">CLG requirement, with exception</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Santa Fe's code</span>
<p>The seven-member Historic Districts Review Board is composed entirely by occupation category — one architect, one historian, one construction-industry representative, one in-district business owner, one Old Santa Fe Association member, and two at-large seats — with no credentialing requirement attached to any of them (LDC §14-2.6(D)(1)).</p>
<span class="field-label">NM CLG standard</span>
<p>HPD's CLG Manual calls for "at least two (2) preservation-related professional members (this includes the professions of architecture, historic architecture, architectural history, archaeology, planning, real estate, design, building trades, landscape architecture, law or finance), to the extent that these professionals are available in the community" (p. 12) — with a documented-search fallback ("how long the position was made available," p. 14) if no qualified volunteer can be found.</p>
<span class="field-label">Internal contrast</span>
<p>Santa Fe's code already has a template for this a few sections away: the city's own Archaeological Review Committee requires its archaeologist and historian seats to hold specific advanced-degree and field-experience minimums, and requires archaeologists to be listed in the state's own preservation-division directory (LDC §14-2.7(D)–(E)). The HDRB's parallel seats — architect, historian — carry no equivalent standard.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row">
<a href="https://library.municode.com/nm/santa_fe/codes/code_of_ordinances?nodeId=CH14LADE_ART14-2REDEKIBO">Santa Fe LDC, Art. 14-2 (Review and Decision-Making Bodies) ↗</a>
<a href="https://nmhistoricpreservation.org/assets/files/clg/NM%20CLG%20Manual%20-%202020.pdf">NM HPD, CLG Manual (2020), pp. 12–14 ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.nps.gov/articles/sec-standards-prof-quals.htm">NPS, Secretary of the Interior's Professional Qualifications Standards ↗</a>
</div>
</article>
<article class="card case-study" id="hp-shpo-design">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>Design-review guidelines</h3>
<span class="cs-tag">Locally discretionary</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Santa Fe's code</span>
<p>The design standards for new construction and alterations in the historic districts — wall-dominated massing, allowed and prohibited roof forms, streetscape-averaged height limits, adobe-toned roof colors, and similar rules tailored to "Old Santa Fe Style" and "Recent Santa Fe Style" — are locally authored and specific (LDC §14-5.2(D)–(I)). Neither Article 14-3 (Review and Approval Procedures) nor Article 14-5 (Overlay Zoning Districts) references the Secretary of the Interior's Standards anywhere in its text.</p>
<span class="field-label">NM CLG standard</span>
<p>The CLG Manual treats this as an open local choice rather than a certification requirement: "Communities can decide what type of guidelines to use but the SOI Standards are suggested" (p. 10), and recommends written guidelines "use the SOI Standards at a minimum" (p. 13).</p>
<span class="field-label">How they compare</span>
<p>Because this is a suggested practice rather than a certification requirement, the absence of a Secretary of the Interior's Standards reference isn't a compliance gap. It is, though, a specific and low-cost thing this LDC update could add: naming the SOI Standards for Rehabilitation as an interpretive backstop for situations Santa Fe's own prescriptive rules don't yet anticipate, without displacing those rules.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row">
<a href="https://library.municode.com/nm/santa_fe/codes/code_of_ordinances?nodeId=CH14LADE_ART14-5OVZODI">Santa Fe LDC, Art. 14-5 (Overlay Zoning Districts) ↗</a>
<a href="https://nmhistoricpreservation.org/assets/files/clg/NM%20CLG%20Manual%20-%202020.pdf">NM HPD, CLG Manual (2020), pp. 10, 13 ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.nps.gov/subjects/taxincentives/secretarys-standards-rehabilitation.htm">NPS, Secretary of the Interior's Standards for Rehabilitation ↗</a>
</div>
</article>
<article class="card case-study" id="hp-shpo-survey">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>Survey &amp; inventory currency</h3>
<span class="cs-tag">CLG requirement</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Santa Fe's own findings</span>
<p>As <a href="/research/historic-preservation/#hp-findings">Santa Fe's own 2021 report documents</a>, the city's systematic surveys of its five historic districts stopped after 2002 — 24 years ago as of this writing, with property-by-property documentation falling to owners as designation or review comes up since.</p>
<span class="field-label">NM CLG standard</span>
<p>This one is a certification requirement, not a suggestion: "CLGs must develop or have in place a system for the survey and inventory of cultural resources within their jurisdictions... in compliance with the Secretary of the Interior's Standards for Identification and Evaluation," updated "at regular intervals (suggested: every 5-10 years) as new resources become eligible for consideration and properties change over time" (p. 15).</p>
<span class="field-label">How they compare</span>
<p>A 24-year gap runs two to five times past the manual's own suggested update window, on a maintenance obligation the CLG Manual frames as a baseline requirement of certification rather than an optional practice — the sharpest of the five gaps in this section for that reason.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row">
<a href="/research/historic-preservation/#hp-findings">2021 Continuity and Connection Report (this page, above) ↗</a>
<a href="https://nmhistoricpreservation.org/assets/files/clg/NM%20CLG%20Manual%20-%202020.pdf">NM HPD, CLG Manual (2020), p. 15 ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.nps.gov/articles/sec_stds_id_standards.htm">NPS, Standards for Identification ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.nps.gov/articles/sec_stds_eval_stds.htm">NPS, Standards for Evaluation ↗</a>
</div>
</article>
<article class="card case-study" id="hp-shpo-demolition">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>Demolition review standards</h3>
<span class="cs-tag">Locally discretionary</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Santa Fe's code</span>
<p>A demolition request in a historic district is weighed against three factors — historical importance, role in a unique street section or block front, and structural condition — within an up-to-65-day review window; there is no economic-hardship allowance built into the standard (LDC §14-3.14(G)).</p>
<span class="field-label">NM CLG standard</span>
<p>Hardship allowances aren't required for CLG certification, but the manual's own ordinance checklist recommends them: "Allowances for financial hardship. No. Yes, with clear documentation requirements to avoid misuse" (p. 11).</p>
<span class="field-label">How they compare</span>
<p>Again a discretionary item, not a compliance gap. If a hardship provision is added, the manual's own framing suggests pairing it with the documentation safeguard it names in the same line — not adding one without the other.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row">
<a href="https://library.municode.com/nm/santa_fe/codes/code_of_ordinances?nodeId=CH14LADE_ART14-3REAPPR">Santa Fe LDC, Art. 14-3 (Review and Approval Procedures) ↗</a>
<a href="https://nmhistoricpreservation.org/assets/files/clg/NM%20CLG%20Manual%20-%202020.pdf">NM HPD, CLG Manual (2020), p. 11 ↗</a>
</div>
</article>
<article class="card case-study" id="hp-shpo-significance">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>Definition of "significance"</h3>
<span class="cs-tag">Drafting, not compliance</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Santa Fe's code</span>
<p>The 2021 report states the code "never formally defines 'significance' at all" — but Article 14-12 (Definitions) does define SIGNIFICANT STRUCTURE, with criteria that loosely track the National Register's own Criteria A, B, and C (association with important events or persons, or listing/eligibility for the State or National Register). The general term SIGNIFICANCE, however, is defined only for archaeological sites, not for the building-designation process the report is describing.</p>
<span class="field-label">NM CLG standard</span>
<p>The CLG Manual doesn't prescribe specific definitional language; it ties survey and evaluation criteria to being "consistent with the National Register" and, for surveys specifically, "in compliance with the Secretary of the Interior's Standards for Identification and Evaluation" (p. 15).</p>
<span class="field-label">How they compare</span>
<p>The report's underlying concern — unclear or untrained application of significance criteria — is likely traceable to this narrower, more specific gap: a defined term (SIGNIFICANT STRUCTURE) that substantively works, sitting next to an undefined general term (SIGNIFICANCE, outside archaeology) that boards and staff are still expected to apply. Naming SIGNIFICANT STRUCTURE's criteria as consistent with the Secretary of the Interior's Standards for Identification and Evaluation, and giving the general term a cross-reference rather than leaving it undefined, would close the gap without changing what already substantively works.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row">
<a href="https://library.municode.com/nm/santa_fe/codes/code_of_ordinances?nodeId=CH14LADE">Santa Fe LDC, Art. 14-12 (Definitions), via Chapter 14 index ↗</a>
<a href="https://nmhistoricpreservation.org/assets/files/clg/NM%20CLG%20Manual%20-%202020.pdf">NM HPD, CLG Manual (2020), p. 15 ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.nps.gov/articles/sec_stds_eval_stds.htm">NPS, Standards for Evaluation ↗</a>
</div>
</article>
